1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,522
In a tug of war, the stronger
team pulls the rope its way.

2
00:00:04,522 --> 00:00:07,452
A magnet has a north end and a south end.

3
00:00:07,452 --> 00:00:10,058
Oil floats on water and will not mix.

4
00:00:10,458 --> 00:00:12,189
Unequal pull, two ends.

5
00:00:12,189 --> 00:00:14,437
Chemical bonds can have this too.

6
00:00:14,887 --> 00:00:18,081
Diya pours a little oil
into water in the kitchen.

7
00:00:18,081 --> 00:00:22,075
Her book says a water molecule
has a plus end and a minus end.

8
00:00:22,425 --> 00:00:26,055
But carbon dioxide also has
strong pulling oxygen atoms.

9
00:00:26,055 --> 00:00:28,411
Why does it have no ends at all?

10
00:00:28,911 --> 00:00:32,639
By the end, you will know how to
add up the pulls in any molecule.

11
00:00:33,289 --> 00:00:34,864
First, a quick reminder.

12
00:00:34,864 --> 00:00:38,798
Electronegativity is how strongly an
atom pulls a shared pair of electrons

13
00:00:38,798 --> 00:00:39,784
towards itself.

14
00:00:40,134 --> 00:00:44,189
When two same atoms share,
like H two, the pull is equal.

15
00:00:44,189 --> 00:00:46,217
The pair sits in the middle.

16
00:00:46,217 --> 00:00:48,497
This is a nonpolar covalent bond.

17
00:00:48,847 --> 00:00:52,421
When the atoms differ, like
HF, fluorine pulls harder.

18
00:00:52,421 --> 00:00:54,490
The pair shifts towards fluorine.

19
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:56,474
This is a polar covalent bond.

20
00:00:56,824 --> 00:00:59,715
Fluorine becomes slightly
negative, delta minus.

21
00:00:59,715 --> 00:01:02,560
Hydrogen becomes slightly
positive, delta plus.

22
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:04,911
Two charged ends make a dipole.

23
00:01:04,911 --> 00:01:06,913
Di means two, pole means end.

24
00:01:07,563 --> 00:01:09,999
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

25
00:01:09,999 --> 00:01:12,278
Two hydrogen atoms share one pair.

26
00:01:12,278 --> 00:01:14,359
It sits exactly in the middle.

27
00:01:14,709 --> 00:01:16,622
Swap one hydrogen for fluorine.

28
00:01:16,622 --> 00:01:18,909
Watch the pair slide towards fluorine.

29
00:01:19,309 --> 00:01:21,431
Now each end has a small charge.

30
00:01:21,431 --> 00:01:26,031
We draw an arrow, from the plus end
to the minus end, with a cross at the

31
00:01:26,031 --> 00:01:26,931
plus tail.

32
00:01:27,244 --> 00:01:28,839
How big is a dipole?

33
00:01:28,839 --> 00:01:33,221
The dipole moment is charge times
the distance between the charges.

34
00:01:33,221 --> 00:01:34,599
Its symbol is mu.

35
00:01:34,999 --> 00:01:37,080
Its unit is the debye, D.

36
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:41,963
One debye is three point three three
five six four times ten to the minus

37
00:01:41,963 --> 00:01:43,535
thirty coulomb metre.

38
00:01:43,935 --> 00:01:47,522
A dipole moment has a size
and a direction, like a push.

39
00:01:47,522 --> 00:01:50,868
So in a molecule, we add
the bond pulls like arrows.

40
00:01:51,268 --> 00:01:54,434
In carbon dioxide, the
molecule is straight.

41
00:01:54,434 --> 00:01:57,719
Two equal pulls point
opposite ways and cancel.

42
00:01:57,719 --> 00:01:59,201
The total is zero.

43
00:01:59,851 --> 00:02:03,090
Compare carbon dioxide
and water side by side.

44
00:02:03,090 --> 00:02:05,068
Both have two polar bonds.

45
00:02:05,418 --> 00:02:06,571
Pause and think.

46
00:02:06,571 --> 00:02:08,548
Which one has a dipole moment?

47
00:02:08,548 --> 00:02:10,000
CO two, or water?

48
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:14,100
Water!

49
00:02:13,757 --> 00:02:16,589
It is bent, so the two
pulls do not cancel.

50
00:02:16,589 --> 00:02:19,229
They add up to one point eight five debye.

51
00:02:19,879 --> 00:02:21,952
Now, shape matters even more.

52
00:02:21,952 --> 00:02:23,019
Step by step.

53
00:02:23,369 --> 00:02:24,269
Step one.

54
00:02:24,225 --> 00:02:27,284
Be F two is straight, like carbon dioxide.

55
00:02:27,284 --> 00:02:28,620
Two pulls cancel.

56
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:29,520
Zero.

57
00:02:29,955 --> 00:02:30,855
Step two.

58
00:02:30,846 --> 00:02:33,151
BF three is a flat triangle.

59
00:02:33,151 --> 00:02:36,107
Three equal pulls at equal angles cancel.

60
00:02:36,107 --> 00:02:37,107
Zero again.

61
00:02:37,857 --> 00:02:38,781
Step three.

62
00:02:38,781 --> 00:02:42,508
Ammonia, NH three, and NF
three are both pyramids.

63
00:02:42,508 --> 00:02:46,185
Nitrogen has one lone pair,
a pair it does not share.

64
00:02:46,935 --> 00:02:47,835
Step four.

65
00:02:47,771 --> 00:02:52,233
In ammonia, the lone pair pull and
the bond pulls point the same way.

66
00:02:52,233 --> 00:02:53,133
They add.

67
00:02:53,021 --> 00:02:57,079
Four point nine zero times ten to
the minus thirty coulomb metre.

68
00:02:57,829 --> 00:03:02,504
In NF three, the bond pulls point
towards fluorine, against the lone pair.

69
00:03:02,504 --> 00:03:03,783
They partly cancel.

70
00:03:03,783 --> 00:03:06,983
Only zero point eight times
ten to the minus thirty.

71
00:03:07,733 --> 00:03:10,692
And ionic bonds can be a little covalent.

72
00:03:10,692 --> 00:03:15,471
A small cation pulls the electron
cloud of a big anion out of shape.

73
00:03:15,471 --> 00:03:17,046
This is polarisation.

74
00:03:17,696 --> 00:03:18,698
Remember this.

75
00:03:18,698 --> 00:03:21,685
Polar bonds do not always
make a polar molecule.

76
00:03:21,685 --> 00:03:22,687
Shape decides.

77
00:03:23,387 --> 00:03:25,732
Now, Fajans' rules,
and one worked example.

78
00:03:26,032 --> 00:03:26,932
Rule one.

79
00:03:26,868 --> 00:03:31,107
A smaller cation and a bigger
anion give more covalent character.

80
00:03:31,607 --> 00:03:32,507
Rule two.

81
00:03:32,393 --> 00:03:36,824
A cation with a bigger charge pulls
harder, so more covalent character.

82
00:03:37,574 --> 00:03:38,533
Rule three.

83
00:03:38,533 --> 00:03:43,307
For the same size and charge, a
transition metal cation pulls harder than

84
00:03:43,307 --> 00:03:45,350
one with a noble gas setup.

85
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:47,765
So, Li Cl or Na Cl?

86
00:03:47,765 --> 00:03:49,410
Lithium ion is smaller.

87
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:51,677
It pulls the chloride cloud more.

88
00:03:51,677 --> 00:03:53,382
Li Cl is more covalent.

89
00:03:54,032 --> 00:03:55,493
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:03:55,843 --> 00:04:00,345
Some students think a molecule with
polar bonds must have a dipole moment.

91
00:04:00,745 --> 00:04:01,645
Not true.

92
00:04:01,584 --> 00:04:06,712
CO two, BF three and methane all have
polar bonds, but their shapes cancel

93
00:04:06,712 --> 00:04:07,612
the pulls.

94
00:04:07,603 --> 00:04:09,599
Their dipole moment is zero.

95
00:04:10,249 --> 00:04:11,675
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:11,675 --> 00:04:15,897
Pick the polar or nonpolar molecule,
compare dipole moments, and order

97
00:04:15,897 --> 00:04:18,098
covalent character by Fajans' rules.

98
00:04:18,698 --> 00:04:19,908
Exam level now.

99
00:04:19,908 --> 00:04:23,699
In which set does every molecule
have zero dipole moment?

100
00:04:23,699 --> 00:04:24,960
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:31,697
The answer is A.

102
00:04:31,697 --> 00:04:34,164
CO two, BF three and methane.

103
00:04:34,664 --> 00:04:40,052
CO two is straight, BF three is a
flat triangle, methane is a perfect

104
00:04:40,052 --> 00:04:41,082
tetrahedron.

105
00:04:41,082 --> 00:04:42,531
All pulls cancel.

106
00:04:43,031 --> 00:04:47,161
Water and ammonia have lone
pairs and bent or pyramid shapes.

107
00:04:47,161 --> 00:04:48,392
They are polar.

108
00:04:48,892 --> 00:04:51,418
NF three is weak, but not zero.

109
00:04:51,418 --> 00:04:54,464
So every other set hides
one polar molecule.

110
00:04:55,064 --> 00:04:56,605
Here is your recap.

111
00:04:56,605 --> 00:04:58,189
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:01,389 --> 00:05:03,575
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

113
00:05:03,975 --> 00:05:04,875
One.

114
00:05:04,511 --> 00:05:08,440
Why is the HF bond polar,
but the HH bond is not?

115
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:10,140
Two.

116
00:05:09,730 --> 00:05:14,207
Why is the dipole moment of water
not zero, but that of CO two is?

117
00:05:15,007 --> 00:05:15,907
Three.

118
00:05:15,522 --> 00:05:17,918
Why is ammonia more polar than NF three?

119
00:05:18,818 --> 00:05:22,486
Next time: why is water bent,
but carbon dioxide straight?

120
00:05:22,486 --> 00:05:26,098
We find out in VSEPR theory
and shapes of molecules.
